/*
	RMSTEST 3 — omit set_gaia_object_only under a reference frame: nothing
	placed, or placed and owned?

	guide:2354 says set_gaia_object_only "Must be used when placing player's
	gold/stone/berries/deer/boar", and guide:2262 says objects that cannot be
	owned by players "also require set_gaia_object_only to be placed for every
	player". docs/preview-design.md Sec.6.6 reads "must"/"require" literally:
	zero placements, one gaiaOnlyRequired failure, one note. Sec.15 item 9
	flags it as unconfirmed, and it matters because 32 of our 33 corpus maps
	use the attribute — so the question is what happens to the ONE that
	forgets. An empty map or a normal one?

	HOW IT DISCRIMINATES
	Two per-player resource commands in separate distance bands so they cannot
	be confused for each other:
	  GOLD  6-10 tiles from the town centre, WITH the flag    -> control
	  STONE 14-18 tiles from the town centre, WITHOUT it      -> the test

	READ IT LIKE THIS
	  Gold ring present, NO stone anywhere
	      -> the engine places nothing. Sec.6.6 confirmed, and its
	         gaiaOnlyRequired failure bucket is the right design.
	  Both rings present
	      -> the attribute is not actually required for placement. Sec.6.6's
	         rule is WRONG and would blank out resources the engine draws
	         fine, which is the opposite of the honest-approximation goal.
	  Both present but stone is player-COLOURED (not neutral grey)
	      -> it places and assigns ownership. Report the colour; that is a
	         third outcome the spec does not model at all.

	RUN ON: Tiny, 2 players. Two town centres, easy to inspect both.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
base_terrain GRASS

create_player_lands
{
	terrain_type GRASS
	land_percent 60
	base_size 8
	circle_radius 30
}

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

/* CONTROL: has the flag. Must appear. */
create_object GOLD
{
	number_of_objects 5
	set_gaia_object_only
	set_place_for_every_player
	min_distance_to_players 6
	max_distance_to_players 10
}

/* THE TEST: identical, but no set_gaia_object_only. */
create_object STONE
{
	number_of_objects 5
	set_place_for_every_player
	min_distance_to_players 14
	max_distance_to_players 18
}
